~
+A selected online candidate is learned under the typed reading only when every character is Han and reads as its typed syllable in the dictionary's single-character tables (characters absent from those tables are accepted); otherwise it is committed without being learned. Umlaut spellings are stored under the dictionary's `nve`/`lve`/`ju` keys.
+
+## Personal context learning
+
+With learning enabled, quanpin and shuangpin commits teach a personal word-sequence model: which word the user committed after which one or two words. It is stored in two journal tables, `personal_bigram` and `personal_trigram`, keyed by word text, so it survives dictionary generations without replay. It is deliberately not part of the dictionary-state export (see `dictionary-state.md`). Deleting a pinyin candidate, or removing a personal pinyin entry, deletes every personal row mentioning that word in the same transaction.
+
+What is recorded: a picked dictionary row counts twice, each word of an accepted whole sentence counts once, and a user word of three or more characters that answers the whole composition (normally a sentence stored by sentence learning) ends the chain without being counted. Other sources (English, emoji, cloud and AI suggestions, local modes, wubi, Japanese) end the chain. Punctuation, raw or reading commits, first/last-character selection, scheme switches, `reset_state`, dedicated English, local modes, nine-key commits and `Session::reset_context()` end it too. Punctuation the engine leaves to the host (English punctuation, a punctuation lock, an untranslated key), and any command or non-pinyin key that arrives with nothing composed (Backspace, Enter, caret moves, digits, spaces) end it on a best-effort basis, since many hosts never forward those keys. A new composition that starts more than 8 seconds after the last commit starts a new chain, which covers hosts that do not report focus changes; hosts should still call `reset_context()` on focus or application changes. Cancel keeps the chain.
+
+The model is used in two places. The sentence lattice adds a bounded per-transition term inside a composition (see `WordLatticeOptions::personal_weight`). After a previous word, the leading group of same-length dictionary homophones is reordered by blending their weight share with the personal probability; fixed positions still win. At the chain start nothing is reordered: context-free preference belongs to the frequency setting, and with the default `FrequencyAdjustmentMode::Disabled` the personal model is the only word-level learning a `Session` does, and only after a previous word.
+
+Frequency learning, pinning and the index-0 check read the order before this reordering, so one context's temporary order is never written into context-free weights and cannot trigger a weight rebalance.
+
+The in-memory model of each journal path lives for the process and keeps its own SQLite connection for writes; one writer mutex covers each database transaction together with its in-memory update. `close_default_user_database()` closes those connections and marks every model stale; the next access reloads from disk outside the read lock and swaps the counts in. When distinct pairs plus triples exceed 200,000, all counts are halved and zeros dropped, repeatedly within one transaction until at most 150,000 entries remain. A load that fails (a busy, damaged or unreadable journal) keeps the previous counts and is retried on a later access, at most every 10 seconds.
+
+## Pick pair words
+
+Picks inside one composition are stored as a phrase when the composition completes. Across compositions, an explicit dictionary pick that opens a composition right after an explicit dictionary pick that ended the previous one is counted in the journal table `pick_transitions`; the third occurrence of the same pair stores the concatenation as a user word under the joined canonical reading, journaled as a user insert so it replays, and drops the counter. Only pairs of at most four Han characters with one syllable per character qualify, and a pair whose word the user deleted (a journal tombstone) is never recreated. Decoded sentences, online, English, emoji, local-mode, wubi and Japanese commits, and commits while learning is disabled, are not picks and end the pair. The pair also ends wherever the personal context chain ends, when a host flushes the composition (`Session::finish`, punctuation), and when the second pick comes more than 10 seconds after the first. Hosts should call `Session::reset_context()` on focus changes, while their own English mode passes keys through, on modifier shortcuts and before inserting text of their own; the engine-side breaks only see keys the host forwards. The table keeps at most 20,000 pairs, is local evidence and is deliberately not part of the dictionary-state export.
+
+## Learnable typo correction
+
+`SessionOptions::autocorrect_types` gates correction with bit 0 (an adjacent transposition) and bit 1 (a neighbouring QWERTY key). Either one on also enables the deletion and insertion bits (`autocorrect_types_from_request`), so a missing or extra letter is corrected whenever correction is on at all; `quanpin::kAutocorrectMissingOrExtra` names those two bits together. The static whole-input tables only rewrite spellings that are not legal pinyin. For an input of three or more complete syllables, every enabled kind also lets the lattice read one typed syllable as a one-edit typo of another legal syllable (`quanpin/syllable_typos.h`), so `meiganxi` can offer 没关系 as well as 没干系. The typo edges are decoded in a second pass over the literal graph, Google sentence or not; syllables the best literal path spells as lone characters are tried first, two- and three-syllable dictionary words spanning them are looked up (a lone replaced character almost never beats the literal reading, and its lookup was the expensive half), and at most 96 variant keys are considered per query. The typo pass runs with a beam of 8, which measured the same as the full beam. A typo sentence is added only when the extended graph's best path uses a typo edge and reads differently from the literal sentence; it is placed after the whole-sentence rows and carries `corrected_from` and the corrected `canonical_pinyin`. It would take the leading seat only when it beats the literal sentence and the fallback by `typo_lead_margin` per typo edge; the margin ships infinite, because every finite margin tried moved the top-1 of a clean eval set.
+
+Selecting a typo sentence stores it under the corrected reading like any other sentence and counts each corrected syllable pair in the journal table `pinyin_typo_counts`. A counted pair's edge cost is discounted by `base / 5 * min(ln(1 + count), 3)`, never below 40% of the base cost. Committing the raw letters (`Command::CommitRaw`) while a candidate offers a correction records the exact folded input in `pinyin_autocorrect_suppressions`; from then on that input is answered with every correction bit cleared and cut without the correction aliases, so neither the static tables, the aliases (`sahng` stays `sa'h'n'g`) nor typo sentences rewrite it. Only the exact input is affected: `sahngzhi` is still corrected after `sahng` was suppressed. Nothing is recorded with learning off or with the mask 0. Hosts that offer "clear learning data" should include both tables.
+
+Both tables hold at most 2,048 rows, trimmed by lowest count and then age in the writing transaction, and are shared by every session on the journal through `quanpin::PersonalTypoProfile`. Each query with a correction bit on compares the journal's and its WAL's modification time and size with the last load and reloads only when they changed; the dictionary's candidate caches are dropped only when the reloaded state differs. The series cache key includes the correction mask, so a list built under one mask is never served under another. The tables are device-local: replay leaves them alone, they survive resource upgrades with the journal, and they are not part of the dictionary-state export.
+
+## Learning undo
+
+With `SessionOptions::learning_undo` (off by default), deleting a commit with Backspace and picking a different candidate for the same input takes back what the deleted commit taught: the frequency rows and selection counters it wrote, a phrase it stored (sentence learning, segmented picks or a pick pair word), its pick pair counter and its personal context counts. With the default `FrequencyAdjustmentMode::Disabled` the frequency part is empty, so what is taken back is the stored phrase and the context counts.
+
+Frequency learning writes absolute weights and rebalances neighbouring rows, so it cannot be undone by subtracting anything. Each learning writer instead reads every row it writes before and after the write, inside its own write transaction and on the connections it already holds, and the commit keeps those images in session memory. The undo runs before the new pick learns, puts every row back to its image before the commit in one transaction over the working dictionary with the journal attached, and then queries the candidates again so the new pick is ranked against the restored weights. A journal row that did not exist is deleted rather than written as a `delete` operation, and one that did exist, a tombstone included, is put back with its `updated_at`, so replay order and dictionary-state export see a state that really existed. If any row changed after the commit (another session, a pin, a removal, an import), nothing is put back; the personal context counts, which are plain counts, are subtracted either way. Nothing new is persisted and there is no schema change.
+
+The session keeps the last four commits and charges each idle `Backspace` to the newest one that still has characters left; deleting past all of them forgets them. A pick whose consumed input starts the same way as a fully deleted commit's, under the same scheme, undoes that commit unless it picks the same word; any other pick forgets the fully deleted commits. The consumed input is the typed raw prefix the pick drains, without apostrophes or helpcodes, so an abbreviation, an autocorrected spelling or a segment of a longer composition matches what was typed. Retyping only the start of a deleted commit and picking a segment is enough, and a phrase stored when a segmented composition completes belongs to its last segment, so it is taken back by retyping that segment's input.
+
+Punctuation, raw and reading commits, first/last-character selection, nine-key text, an idle Space (`CommitCandidate`) and printable keys the engine leaves to the host count as inserted text without anything to undo. Idle Enter, Escape, caret moves and forward deletion, scheme and English-mode switches, `reset_state`, `reset_context()`, `forget_recent_commits()`, switching the nine-key grid and any successful pin, removal or fixed-position edit forget the recent commits. All idle keys still return unhandled, so host key handling does not change. A host that enables the option must forward idle Backspace, Space, Enter and caret keys and call `forget_recent_commits()` (or `reset_context()`) whenever the text near the caret changes without passing through a `KeyResult`. A restore that fails surfaces as the selecting call's diagnostic.
+
